WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.889
A single durian can lock an entire growing zone out of its export market.

2
00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:10.198
In 2022, Vietnamese durian won the right to be sold officially to China.

3
00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:15.969
Each orchard needs a growing-area code, each packing house its own: a passport for the fruit.

4
00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:20.890
Mr. Tư in Cái Bè has a small orchard, so he shares one code with his neighbours.

5
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:26.748
In three years, exports leapt from 124 million dollars to 3.3 billion.

6
00:00:26.778 --> 00:00:30.513
Then thirty shipments were flagged for cadmium, a heavy metal.

7
00:00:30.543 --> 00:00:41.539
From January 2025, every shipment also needed a test certificate for Auramine O, an industrial dye. Within two weeks, hundreds of containers were turned back.

8
00:00:41.569 --> 00:00:48.809
Codes became scarce. Businesses told reporters that a single code had changed hands for about 120 million dong.

9
00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:56.573
In November 2025, police charged seventeen people after uncovering a trade in growing-area codes and test certificates.

10
00:00:56.603 --> 00:01:05.765
And if one fruit in a shipment carries cadmium or Auramine O, the whole shipment goes back, the zone's code is suspended, and Mr. Tư's family pays too.

11
00:01:05.795 --> 00:01:14.455
Luckily, at first, Auramine O was only found in Thai durian. Unluckily, the new rule applied to durian from every country.

12
00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:25.650
You see, a code is like an exam number: one wrong paper, one number marked down. But once someone sits the exam for someone else, the proctor searches every pocket in the room.

13
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:33.849
So now every shipment needs a growing-area code, a packing-house code and a test certificate. Compliance is the price of the ticket.

14
00:01:33.879 --> 00:01:40.900
On the other side of the border, nobody tastes Mr. Tư's fruit. They only read one line: Viet Nam.
